{"name":"Mermaid Blues","hex":"#004477","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mermaid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythological creature with a woman's head and upper body, and a tail of a fish.","Coloured a brilliant turquoise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mermaid"},{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mermaid","blocks":[{"text":"In folklore, a mermaid is an aquatic creature with the head and upper body of a female human and the tail of a fish. Mermaids appear in the folklore of many cultures worldwide, including Europe, Latin America, Asia, and Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/John_William_Waterhouse_A_Mermaid.jpg/330px-John_William_Waterhouse_A_Mermaid.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mermaid","wikidata":"Q182559"},{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues","wikidata":"Q9759"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Leighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg/500px-Leighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Leighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg/1280px-Leighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Leighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg/500px-Leighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Fisherman and the Syren, by Frederic Leighton, c. 1856–1858","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeighton-The_Fisherman_and_the_Syren-c._1856-1858.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg/500px-Bern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg/1280px-Bern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg/500px-Bern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABern_Cod.318%2C_f.013v-de_natura_serenae_et_honocentauris.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/BL-Add_11283%2C_fo.020v-siren.jpg/500px-BL-Add_11